\begin{abstract}
The dominant deep-learning paradigm computes a fixed-cost function, capping accuracy at model capacity rather than available test-time budget. Energy-based models (EBMs) offer an alternative---iterative gradient descent on a learned energy---but global-flatten MLP-EBMs create shallow, poorly-structured basins that fail to support beneficial iteration, plateauing at $K{=}1$. We study \textbf{KAN-EBM}, an architecture that pairs a convolutional filter bank with a Kolmogorov-Arnold Network as a B-spline-parameterized per-pixel energy density. Landscape geometry analysis reveals KAN creates deep, pronounced basins with sharp walls---$\sim$2$\times$ higher energy barriers and Hessian curvature than ConvMLP controls---providing strong gradient signals that sustain iteration. KAN-EBM occupies the \emph{low-parameter, high-FLOP} corner: on CIFAR-10, a 110K-parameter model achieves $37.8{\times}$ higher PSNR-per-parameter than a 3.7M FFN baseline, although it is $53{\times}$ less FLOP-efficient at peak quality. We additionally compare to a matched-parameter CNN denoiser (micro-DnCNN, $\sim$8K params) and report both PSNR and SSIM on 500 test images. The paper's main empirical contribution is the scaling law $K^{*}(\sigma) \approx 86.7\,\sigma^{1.53}$ (bootstrap 95\% CI on $\alpha$: $[1.35, 1.73]$, $R^{2}{=}0.98$), reproducible across datasets, architecture sizes, and B-spline orders, with a precise falsifiable boundary (Gaussian deblurring). A matched-architecture activation ablation (\cref{sec:smooth-control}) shows that the K* phenomenon is a property of the conv-backbone + per-pixel local-energy formulation: it is reproduced \emph{identically} by smooth (GELU, SiLU, Tanh) and rough (ReLU) MLP heads at matched parameters ($\alpha{=}1.26$ for all four). The KAN head's distinct contribution is the \emph{largest} exponent ($\Delta\alpha = +0.27$ over ConvMLP, 95\% CI $[+0.07, +0.46]$, excluding zero) and a $+0.77$\,dB advantage in peak PSNR at matched architecture. The law gives a calibrated, search-free knob for trading inference compute for quality.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Deep learning typically trades accuracy for parameter count \citep{kaplan2020scaling,hoffmann2022chinchilla}. While language models have begun leveraging test-time compute (e.g., search, chain-of-thought) to match larger models \citep{snell2024llm}, vision models remain largely one-shot. Energy-based models (EBMs) \citep{lecun2006ebm} define an energy $E_\theta(\xx)$ and run gradient descent $\xx_{t+1} = \xx_t - \eta\nabla E_\theta(\xx_t)$ at inference, allowing quality to improve with step count $K$. This paradigm has deep roots in statistical physics and recurrent associative memory and has seen renewed interest through score-based generative modeling \citep{song2019generative,song2020improved}. Yet global-flatten MLP-EBMs (the standard EBM parameterization for vision \citep{du2019implicit, nijkamp2019anatomy}) typically plateau at $K{=}1$ when small, not because their landscapes lack smoothness, but because they create \emph{shallow, poorly-structured basins} without the deep minima and strong gradient signals needed to sustain iteration \citep{gao2020learning}. We show in \cref{sec:landscape} that KAN-EBM creates well-structured basins---deep minima bounded by sharp walls with $\sim$2$\times$ higher energy barriers and Hessian curvature than ConvMLP controls---and in \cref{sec:smooth-control} that the architectural choice of a conv backbone with per-pixel local energy is the load-bearing factor for the $K^*$ scaling law. This paper characterises the law and identifies KAN-EBM as the empirically strongest member of this class.

