We investigate \textbf{Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks (KANs)} \citep{liu2024kan} as the energy parameterization. KANs replace fixed activation functions with learnable univariate B-splines on network edges, offering a flexible function class with strong approximation guarantees. Our contributions are: (1)~a 32K-parameter KAN-EBM with higher PSNR-per-parameter than larger baselines, while costing $30$--$60{\times}$ more FLOPs at peak quality (we report both axes); (2)~the empirical scaling law $K^{*}(\sigma) \approx C\sigma^{\alpha}$ with $\alpha{\approx}1.5$ for KAN-EBM, with bootstrap CIs, reproducible across datasets, architecture sizes, and B-spline orders, and with a precise falsifiable boundary on Gaussian deblurring; (3)~a matched-architecture activation ablation showing that the K* phenomenon is a property of the conv-backbone + per-pixel local-energy formulation rather than of any specific head: GELU, SiLU, Tanh, and ReLU all give \emph{identical} fits ($\alpha{=}1.26$, $C{=}39.2$, $R^2{=}0.98$). Landscape geometry analysis (\cref{sec:landscape}) reveals KAN creates deep, pronounced basins with $\sim$2$\times$ higher barriers and curvature than ConvMLP controls, explaining why it yields the largest exponent ($\Delta\alpha = +0.27$, 95\% CI $[+0.07, +0.46]$) and $+0.77$\,dB higher peak PSNR. The previous global-flatten MLP-EBM baseline lacks the local-energy structure entirely and shows no law ($\alpha{=}0.29$, $R^2{=}0.42$). (4)~We validate the law on 500 test images (vs.~24 in the original protocol) and compare to a micro-DnCNN baseline at matched parameter count \citep{zhang2017}, confirming that KAN-EBM's distinctive property is controllable test-time scaling rather than single-pass PSNR dominance.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Test-time compute scaling.} Recent work in large language models has shown that allocating additional computation at inference time---via search, verifiers, or longer chain-of-thought reasoning---can match or exceed the gains from scaling model parameters \citep{snell2024llm,brown2024llm_scaling}. In vision, \citet{wu2025inference} demonstrated that diffusion models benefit from inference-time scaling beyond simply increasing denoising steps, using test-time training to adapt the score network. Concurrent work on step scaling for diffusion models \citep{wu2024inference} has explored optimal discretization schedules. KAN-EBM extends this line to gradient-based iterative denoising with an \emph{analytic, noise-conditioned stopping rule} that requires no auxiliary training: the optimal step count is given by a simple power law in the noise level.

\paragraph{Energy-based models for vision.} EBMs have a long history in computer vision, from early Boltzmann machines to modern implicit generative models \citep{du2019implicit, du2021conjunction}. \citet{du2019implicit} showed that global-flatten MLP-EBMs can generate high-quality images when large, but small variants plateau at $K{=}1$ and fail to support iterative refinement. Convolutional EBMs \citep{xie2016theory} improved spatial structure but still struggled with iterative inference depth. We show that the critical missing ingredient is not model capacity per se but \emph{landscape geometry}: the conv-backbone + per-pixel local-energy formulation creates basins that naturally support multi-step descent.

\paragraph{KANs in generative modeling.} Since their introduction \citep{liu2024kan}, KANs have been applied to physics-informed learning \citep{shukla2024physicsinformed}, graph neural networks \citep{kiamari2024gkan}, and transformers \citep{kat2024}. In generative modeling, \citet{raj2025kaem} proposed KAEMs, which use KAN-parameterized univariate energy functions in \emph{latent space} combined with inverse-transform sampling; this is complementary to our approach, which uses per-pixel spatial energy densities with iterative Langevin dynamics. \citet{li2024ukan} introduced U-KAN, the first work combining KANs with diffusion-based generation in a U-Net architecture for medical image segmentation. KAN-EBM is the first to study KAN-parameterized energy landscapes for controllable test-time compute scaling in image restoration, and the first to connect landscape geometry metrics to a predictive scaling law.

\paragraph{Energy landscape geometry.} The geometry of learned energy landscapes has been recognized as central to EBM trainability since \citet{lecun2006ebm}. \citet{dawid2024ebt} proposed Energy-Based Training (EBT) with explicit landscape regularization to encourage flat minima and avoid spurious modes. Modern Hopfield Networks \citep{ramsauer2021hopfield} provided theoretical analysis of energy basins and their storage capacity, characterizing basin radius in terms of pattern separation. \citet{chaudhari2017entropy} introduced local entropy as a regularizer that widens basins and improves generalization. Our work contributes an \emph{empirical} characterization of how landscape geometry (barrier height, Hessian curvature, basin radius) directly determines the scaling exponent of inference depth, connecting geometric properties to a predictive law.

\paragraph{B-spline activations.} Trainable B-spline activation functions have a direct precedent in signal processing: \citet{bohra2020spline} proposed learnable spline activations for neural networks and demonstrated their approximation advantages on regression tasks. KANs extend this idea to a full network architecture where every edge carries an independent learnable spline, with theoretical guarantees from the Kolmogorov-Arnold representation theorem.

\section{KAN-EBM Architecture and Training}
\label{sec:architecture}

\textbf{Architecture.} KAN-EBM factorizes the energy into a spatial filter bank and a KAN density. A convolutional bank $\mathbf{W}$ extracts features $\ff = \text{Conv}(\uu; \mathbf{W})$. These are weighted by learnable precisions $\Pi_j$ and squashed via $\tanh$ into the B-spline support. A shallow KAN computes the local energy: $E(\uu) = \sum_{h,w} \text{KAN}(\tilde\ff_{h,w})$.

The KAN head consists of $L$ layers. In contrast to standard MLPs, where each edge carries a scalar weight and neurons apply a \emph{fixed} nonlinearity (e.g., ReLU, GELU), each edge in a KAN layer implements an \emph{independent learnable univariate function} parameterized as a B-spline. Specifically, the function on edge $(i,j)$ is
\begin{equation}
    \phi_{ij}(x) = \sum_{m=1}^{M} c_{ijm} \, B_m(x),
\end{equation}
where $B_m$ are basis B-splines of order $k$ defined on a uniform grid of $G$ intervals, and $c_{ijm}$ are learnable coefficients. The CIFAR-10 ``small'' instance uses $16$ filters of size $5{\times}5$ and a per-pixel KAN head $[48, 48, 16, 1]$ (B-spline grid $G{=}5$, order $k{=}3$) for $\sim$32K total parameters.

This structure is particularly suitable for energy functions because univariate B-splines can represent complex potential shapes---double wells, asymmetric barriers, and oscillatory patterns---with far fewer parameters than a fixed-activation MLP would require for equivalent expressivity \citep{deboor1978splines, liu2024kan}. Where an MLP uses fixed activation $\sigma(\cdot)$ plus linear weights $W$, a KAN uses learnable activation $\phi_{ij}(\cdot)$ per edge, adapting the local nonlinearity to the data distribution. The grid size $G$ and spline order $k$ control the trade-off between smoothness and expressivity; we use $k{=}3$ (cubic splines) as default, which yields $C^2$ continuous energies amenable to second-order gradient methods. See \citet{liu2024kan} for the theoretical foundations of KAN approximation.

\textbf{Matched-architecture controls.} Throughout we compare to two control families. (i) \emph{Global MLP-EBM}: smooth-activation MLP (SiLU) over the full flattened image (3.2M params on CelebA) \citep{du2019implicit}. (ii) \emph{ConvMLP-EBM} (this work, \cref{sec:smooth-control}): \emph{identical} conv backbone and per-pixel architecture as KAN-EBM, but the KAN head is replaced by a smooth MLP head $[48, 160, 160, 1]$ at matched parameter count ($\sim$35K), with activation $\in \{$SiLU, GELU, Tanh, ReLU$\}$. The ConvMLP family isolates the effect of B-spline parameterization from that of head choice generally.

\textbf{Training and Inference.} Training uses Denoising Score Matching (DSM) \citep{vincent2011dsm} with second-order autograd. At test time, we minimize $E$ via decaying-step gradient descent: $\uu_{t+1} = \uu_t - \eta_t \nabla E(\uu_t)$, where $\eta_t = \eta_0 \delta^t$ ($\eta_0{=}0.05$, $\delta{=}0.97$). The number of steps $K$ is the inference budget.

\paragraph{Algorithmic remark.} Allen-Cahn gradient flow, score following under $p\propto e^{-E}$, and predictive-coding free-energy minimization all reduce to $\dot\uu = -\nabla E(\uu)$ for a $C^2$ energy $E$ \citep{allen1979microscopic, friston2005free}; the contribution of KAN-EBM is the \emph{learned} $E$ rather than the dynamics.

\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Parameter Pareto vs.~Compute Pareto}
\label{sec:pareto}
We report the trade-off along both axes (\cref{fig:pareto-honest}). On CIFAR-10, KAN-EBM (110K params) reaches 30.36\,dB at $K{=}2$ vs.~26.79\,dB for a 3.7M FFN-DSM---$37.8{\times}$ better PSNR-per-parameter but $53{\times}$ worse per-FLOP. On CelebA-64, KAN-EBM (32K) reaches 27.39\,dB at $K{=}5$ vs.~20.56\,dB for a 3.21M smooth-MLP-EBM (\cref{tab:celeba}). \emph{We do not claim FLOP-Pareto-optimality;} the contribution is parameter efficiency plus the K-scaling property (\cref{sec:kstar}).

