\subsection{Matched-architecture activation ablation: where the K* law lives}
\label{sec:smooth-control}
To localise the cause of the K* law, we train four ConvMLP-EBM variants at matched ($\sim$35K) parameters and identical conv backbone, varying only the head activation: GELU, SiLU, Tanh, and ReLU. All are trained on CIFAR-10 with identical DSM hyperparameters and evaluated under the same K* protocol (3 seeds, 24 images per $\sigma$, 5 noise levels). \cref{tab:smooth-mlp} reports the $K^*(\sigma)$ fits.

The result is informative and partly negative for our initial hypothesis. \textbf{(i) The K* law is robust across head activations.} All four ConvMLP variants---including ReLU---produce \emph{identical} $K^*$ sequences $\{1, 2, 3, 5, 10\}$ and identical fits ($\alpha{=}1.26$, $C{=}39.2$, $R^2{=}0.98$). This rules out ``B-spline parameterization is necessary'' and ``activation smoothness is necessary''. \textbf{(ii) Architecture, not head, is the load-bearing factor.} The global-flatten MLP-EBM ($3.2$M params, SiLU) shows no law ($\alpha{=}0.29$, CI $[-0.00, 0.58]$). The discriminating feature is the conv-backbone + per-pixel local-energy structure. \textbf{(iii) The KAN head gives a statistically significantly larger exponent.} Bootstrap yields $\Delta\alpha = +0.271$, 95\% CI $[+0.074, +0.456]$; the KAN head also achieves $+0.77$\,dB higher peak PSNR at $\sigma{=}0.15$. \textbf{(iv) Landscape geometry explains why KAN is the strongest member.} As shown in \cref{sec:landscape}, KAN creates deeper, more pronounced basins with sharper walls and stronger gradients than the ConvMLP variants. The ConvMLP models reproduce the K* law with the same exponent $\alpha$ but shallower basins and lower peak PSNR. We frame KAN-EBM as the strongest member of a class rather than the unique cause of the phenomenon. \emph{Note on fit independence:} identical discrete $K^*$ sequences mathematically yield identical power-law fits; the analysis was independently recomputed per variant (see supplementary material).

