\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Model & $\lambda_{\max}$ & Barrier & Basin Radius & PSNR @ $K{=}10$ \\
\midrule
KAN-EBM & 126 & 3902 & 0.40 & 25.10 \\
ConvMLP-GELU & 60 & 2035 & 0.00 & 22.39 \\
ConvMLP-ReLU & 2 & 1987 & 0.30 & 22.98 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{\textbf{Landscape geometry} on CIFAR-10 ($\sigma{=}0.15$). KAN-EBM shows $\sim$2$\times$ higher Hessian curvature and energy barriers than ConvMLP-GELU, with a pronounced basin radius. All differences are significant at $p<0.001$ with effect size $r>0.8$.}
\label{tab:landscape}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{outputs/landscape_geometry/unified_figures.png}
  \caption{\textbf{Landscape geometry.} KAN-EBM creates deep, pronounced energy basins with sharp walls (left), whereas ConvMLP-GELU produces shallow, fragmented structures (right). The visualisation combines Hessian spectra, barrier heights, and gradient flow analysis.}
  \label{fig:landscape}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Methodology.} We characterize landscape geometry through four complementary measurements. \emph{Hessian curvature:} the maximum eigenvalue $\lambda_{\max}$ of the energy Hessian $\nabla^2 E(\uu)$ at the denoised minimum, estimated via Lanczos iteration with random probe directions. $\lambda_{\max}$ captures the sharpest curvature at the basin floor. \emph{Energy barrier:} we measure the maximum energy increase along a linear interpolation path from the denoised minimum to the noisy initialization, $\max_{t \in [0,1]} E\bigl(t\tilde\uu + (1-t)\uu^*\bigr) - E(\uu^*)$. This quantifies how ``deep'' the basin is relative to the surrounding landscape. \emph{Basin radius:} the maximum initial noise level $\sigma_{\mathrm{init}}$ for which PSNR exceeds 20\,dB after $K{=}20$ gradient steps, averaged over 100 test images. This operational definition captures the practical size of the basin of attraction. \emph{Gradient Lipschitz proxy:} the ratio $\|\nabla E(\tilde\uu)\| / \|\tilde\uu - \uu^*\|$, which bounds the local Lipschitz constant of the gradient and indicates how strongly the landscape ``pulls'' toward the minimum from a given initialization.

\textbf{Discussion of metrics.} A higher $\lambda_{\max}$ (126 for KAN vs.~60 for GELU) means sharper basin walls, which provide stronger gradient signals that guide descent efficiently. A larger energy barrier (3902 vs.~2035) indicates deeper, more pronounced minima that are well-separated from the surrounding landscape, reducing the risk of the optimizer escaping to spurious regions. Basin radius (0.40 vs.~0.00) is perhaps the most operationally meaningful: it measures whether the basin remains coherent when initialized from realistic noise levels. The GELU control essentially lacks a coherent basin at this noise level, while KAN-EBM maintains a robust region of attraction. Finally, the PSNR at $K{=}10$ (25.1\,dB vs.~22.4\,dB, $p<0.001$, $r{=}0.969$) confirms that these geometric advantages translate directly into sustained empirical improvement with iteration.

\textbf{Connection to the $K^*$ law.} The landscape metrics explain \emph{why} KAN-EBM yields a larger scaling exponent. Deeper basins with sharper walls mean that gradient descent remains effective over a wider range of noise levels: at moderate $\sigma$, the initialization still lies within the basin of attraction, and the strong gradients efficiently guide the trajectory toward the minimum. As $\sigma$ increases, the higher barrier and larger basin radius delay the point at which the optimizer fragments or diverges, allowing more steps to remain beneficial. In contrast, shallow landscapes (global-flatten MLP) show no law because there is essentially no basin to descend: one step reaches the flat region, and subsequent steps wander. The ConvMLP variants occupy an intermediate regime: they have sufficient basin structure to support a power law, but the shallower depth and smaller radius limit the exponent to $\alpha{=}1.26$.

\subsection{Scaled evaluation and modern baselines}
\label{sec:scaled}
To address reviewer concerns about evaluation scale and baseline competitiveness, we re-evaluate all CIFAR-10 models on 500 test images (vs.~24 in the original protocol) and add a micro-DnCNN baseline ($\sim$8K params, 5-layer residual CNN \citep{zhang2017}). \textbf{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:scaled500} reports PSNR and SSIM. At low noise ($\sigma{=}0.05$) KAN-EBM dominates (33.8 dB); at medium noise ($\sigma{=}0.1$--$0.2$) the DnCNN achieves higher single-pass PSNR (31.4 dB vs.~30.4 dB at $\sigma{=}0.1$), but lacks test-time scaling. KAN-EBM's distinctive property is not absolute PSNR superiority but the controllable $K$-scaling: quality rises from K=1 to K=5 and degrades gracefully beyond, while DnCNN and FFN-DSM are flat in $K$. On SSIM, KAN-EBM at its peak exceeds both baselines at every noise level. The 500-image $K^*$ sequence is $\{1,2,5,5,10\}$ (vs.~$\{1,2,5,7,15\}$ on 24 images), yielding $\alpha{=}1.29$ ($R^2{=}0.97$, bootstrap CI $[0.94,1.55]$), consistent with the original fit (CIs overlap).

